1. Check your input source: Make sure that you're providing the correct input to the Scanner. For example, if you expect the user to enter an integer value, make sure they're entering only digits and no other characters or whitespace. If you're reading from a file, check that the format of the input matches what you expect.
2. Use try-catch block: Wrap the Scanner call with a try-catch block to handle the InputMismatchException. This will allow you to provide more informative error messages to the user instead of the default one. For example:

```java
Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
int num;
try {
  System.out.print("Enter an integer: ");
  num = scanner.nextInt();
} catch (InputMismatchException e) {
  System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter a valid integer.");
  scanner.nextLine(); // consume the invalid input
}
```

3. Use hasNextInt() method: Before trying to read an integer using nextInt(), use the hasNextInt() method to check whether the input matches an integer. For example:

```java
Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
String input;
int num;
while (true) {
  System.out.print("Enter an integer: ");
  input = scanner.nextLine();
  Scanner lineScanner = new Scanner(new StringReader(input));
  if (lineScanner.hasNextInt()) {
    num = lineScanner.nextInt();
    break;
  } else {
    System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter a valid integer.");
    lineScanner.close();
  }
}
```

This approach allows you to keep asking for input until a valid integer is provided.

I hope these suggestions help you resolve the InputMismatchException error in your Java code.

