 To write a C++ program that implements the bubble sort algorithm with minimal memory usage and error avoidance for sorting an integer array from a JSON object, follow these steps:

1. First, let's focus on implementing the bubble sort algorithm in C++. Here is a simple implementation:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

void bubbleSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = static_cast<int>(arr.size());
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                std::swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> arr = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6}; // Define your array here
    bubbleSort(arr);                             // Call the bubble sort function
    for (const auto& num : arr) {
        std::cout << num << " ";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

This implementation uses a `std::vector` instead of a traditional array since it offers dynamic memory allocation and automatic memory management. This eliminates the need to manually allocate memory and helps minimize potential errors.

