 Certainly! Here's a PHP custom function named `smart_sort_array` that meets your requirements:

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order = 'ASC') { // Set default order as Ascending
    $size = count($set);
    for ($i = 0; $i < $size - 1; $i++) {
        for ($j = 0; $j < $size - $i - 1; $j++) {
            if (($order == 'ASC' && $set[$j]['age'] > $set[$j+1]['age']) || ($order == 'DESC' && $set[$j]['age'] < $set[$j+1]['age'])) {
                $temp = $set[$j];
                $set[$j] = $set[$j+1];
                $set[$j+1] = $temp;
            }
        }
    }
    return $set;
}
```

This function accepts two arguments: the first argument `$set` is an array containing other associative arrays, and the second optional argument `$order` sets the sorting order (default is ascending). This function uses simple switching (bubble sort) algorithm to sort the elements based on their age.

Example usage:

```php
$people = [
    ['name' => 'John Doe', 'address' => 'New York', 'age' => 25],
    ['name' => 'Jane Smith', 'address' => 'Los Angeles', 'age' => 32],
    ['name' => 'Mike Johnson', 'address' => 'Chicago', 'age' => 19],
];

// Sort in ascending order
$sortedPeopleAsc = smart_sort_array($people);
print_r($sortedPeopleAsc);

// Sort in descending order
$sortedPeopleDesc = smart_sort_array($people, 'DESC');
print_r($sortedPeopleDesc);
```

 A JavaScript Promise is an object that represents the eventual completion or failure of an asynchronous operation and its resulting value. In simpler terms, it's like a container for data or error that will be available at some point in the future.

Imagine you order food from a restaurant. When you place your order (making a function call), the kitchen starts preparing it (an asynchronous operation). However, you don't want to wait around the counter the whole time; instead, you go back to your table and do other things. The Promise is like a ticket with your order number on it. When your food is ready, the kitchen staff will give you a call or send a message (resolving the Promise) to come pick up your meal. If there was a problem with your order (e.g., they ran out of an ingredient), they would notify you of this as well (rejecting the Promise).

In code, you create a new promise by using the `new` keyword followed by the Promise constructor:

```javascript
let myPromise = new Promise(function(resolve, reject) {
  // asynchronous operation goes here
});
```

Then, you can use methods like `then()` and `catch()` to handle the resolved or rejected states:

```javascript
myPromise
  .then(function(result) {
    console.log("Success:", result);
  })
  .catch(function(error) {
    console.log("Error:", error);
  });
```

Promises help keep your code cleaner and more readable when dealing with multiple asynchronous operations, as they allow chaining and proper error handling.

 Here's a Javascript function that should help you find all entries in an object where the nested key value matches a given string key, using dot notation. This function takes an object `obj` and a string `key` as arguments:

```javascript
function findNestedEntry(obj, key) {
  // Helper function to traverse the object using dot notation
  function traverseObject(object, currentKey) {
    if (typeof object !== 'object') return; // Base case: not an object

    for (const propName in object) {
      const propertyFullName = currentKey ? `${currentKey}.${propName}` : propName;

      if (propName === key || (typeof object[propName] === 'object' && findNestedEntry(object[propName], propertyFullName))) {
        return object; // Match found
      }
    }

    return null; // No match found in this object
  }

  return traverseObject(obj, null);
}

// Usage example:
const obj = {
  a: { b: { c: 'match' }, d: { e: 'notMatch' } },
  x: { y: { z: 'match' } },
  g: { h: 'match' }
};

console.log(findNestedEntry(obj, 'c')); // { a: { b: { c: 'match' } } }
console.log(findNestedEntry(obj, 'z')); // { x: { y: { z: 'match' } } }
console.log(findNestedEntry(obj, 'e')); // null
```

This function uses recursion and a helper function `traverseObject()` to go through each level of the object using dot notation. The function returns the matching entry when it finds the key-value pair or null when no match is found.

