 To retrieve the username from a JWT (JSON Web Token) in an ASP.NET C# WebApi project, follow these steps:

1. First, make sure you have a `JwtSecurityTokenHandler` instance available for parsing your JWT tokens. If not, add it as a using directive at the top of your file:

```csharp
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt; // For JwtSecurityTokenHandler
```

2. Create an extension method to parse the JWT token and extract the username:

```csharp
public static string GetUsernameFromJwt(this HttpRequestBase request)
{
    if (request == null || string.IsNullOrEmpty(request.Headers["Authorization"])) return null;

    var authHeader = request.Headers["Authorization"].Split(' ');

    if (authHeader.Length < 2 || string.Compare(authHeader[0], "Bearer", true) != 0) return null;

    using (var jwtHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler())
    {
        var jwtToken = jwtHandler.ReadToken(authHeader[1]) as JwtSecurityToken;

        if (jwtToken == null) return null;

        return jwtToken.Claims.FirstOrDefault(x => x.Type == ClaimTypes.Name)?.Value;
    }
}
```

3. Use this extension method within your controller action:

```csharp
[ApiController]
[Route("[controller]")]
public class ValuesController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("GetUsernameFromJwt")]
    public string Get()
    {
        return HttpContext.Request.GetUsernameFromJwt();
    }
}
```

Now when you call the `GET /Values/GetUsernameFromJwt` endpoint, it will automatically extract the username from the JWT token present in the Authorization header and return it as the response.

