In programming, functions are blocks of reusable code that perform specific tasks. There are two types of functions in some programming languages, including C and JavaScript: inner functions and outer functions.

An outer function is the function that is defined at the top level or outside of any other functions. It can be called directly and can have its own variables and parameters. When an outer function is invoked, it creates a new execution context, and all the variables and parameters within it become accessible.

Inner functions, on the other hand, are functions that are defined inside another function. They have access to the variables and parameters of their enclosing outer function, even after the outer function has returned. This feature is known as "function scoping" or "closure." Inner functions can be used to create more modular and encapsulated code, and they can simplify complex logic by abstracting away details.

Here's an example in JavaScript to illustrate the concept:

```javascript
function outerFunction(x) {
  let outerVariable = x;

  function innerFunction() {
    let innerVariable = 2;
    console.log("outerVariable:", outerVariable);
    console.log("innerVariable:", innerVariable);
  }

  return innerFunction;
}

const myOuterFunction = outerFunction(5);
myOuterFunction(); // logs: outerVariable: 5, innerVariable: 2
```

In this example, `outerFunction` is the outer function, which takes an argument `x` and defines an inner function `innerFunction`. The inner function does not take any arguments and simply logs the values of `outerVariable` and itself when called. We then call `outerFunction` with an argument of `5`, which returns the inner function. Finally, we call the inner function, which logs the values of both `outerVariable` and `innerVariable`.

 To create an efficient NoSQL document schema for a hotel room reservation system running on MongoDB with the given information, I'd suggest using the following schema design:

```javascript
// Define the Hotel model
const hotelSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  name: { type: String, required: true }, // Name of the hotel
  location: { type: GeoPoint, index: '2dsphere' }, // Location of the hotel (latitude and longitude)
  rooms: [{ type: Schema.Types.ObjectId, ref: 'Room' }], // Array of references to associated Room documents
});

// Define the Room model
const roomSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  number: { type: Number, required: true, unique: true }, // Unique room number
  capacity: { type: Number, min: 1, max: 4 }, // Maximum number of guests per room
  pricePerNight: { type: Number, required: true }, // Base price per night
  availability: [ // An array of availability objects
    {
      startDate: { type: Date, required: true }, // Start date of the reservation
      endDate: { type: Date, required: true }, // End date of the reservation
      status: { type: String, enum: ['available', 'reserved'], default: 'available' }, // Status of the room during this period
    },
  ],
});

// Create and export the models
const Hotel = mongoose.model('Hotel', hotelSchema);
const Room = mongoose.model('Room', roomSchema);

// Indexes for faster lookups
hotelSchema.index({ name: 'text' }); // Full text search for hotel names
roomSchema.index({ number: 1, availability: { $elemMatch: { startDate: -1 } } }); // Fastest lookup for available rooms by room number and date range
roomSchema.index({ availability: { $elemMatch: { startDate: 1, endDate: 1 } } }, { unique: true }); // Unique index on room availability
